            <ArticleTitle>Surgical Treatment of Inferior Pancreaticoduodenal Artery Aneurysm with Common Hepatic Artery Revascularization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>313.e9-313.e11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0890-5096(16)31289-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.avsg.2017.03.179</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pancreaticoduodenal artery aneurysms (PDAAs) are rarely seen among the visceral artery aneurysms (&lt;2%). PDAA is mostly asymptomatic but can be presented with abdominal pain. Embolization can be applied safely with a high success rate; however, in some conditions such as when embolization fails or accompanying celiac axis occlusion, open surgery can be performed. We report the successful management of a patient with PDAA and occlusion of the celiac axis with uneventful short- and mid-term follow-up.</AbstractText>
